WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.110 --> 00:00:05.370
So I want you to think about the last time
you failed miserably. I want you to think

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:09.580
about the last time you had the worst setback.
I want you to think about the last time something

00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:14.839
didn't go your way. How did you respond to
that? What did you do right afterwards? We

00:00:14.839 --> 00:00:18.340
learn a lot when that takes place. I once
heard a mentor of mine that told me, "You

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:22.340
learn a lot about a human being or yourself
when you have a major setback in your life."

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:25.720
Right? Because the reality part of it is,
when I'm about to tell you about small thinkers

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:30.210
is the basis of it, before I give you the
answer, the basis of it is small thinkers

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:34.590
have one enemy. It isn't their competition.
It isn't their parents. It isn't their girlfriend.

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:37.430
It isn't their spouse. It isn't their best
friend. It isn't their staff. It isn't their

00:00:37.430 --> 00:00:41.390
customers. It isn't the market. They have
one enemy, and it's their mindset and the

00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:45.940
way they think. That is the biggest problem
with small thinkers. So what is the one thing

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:53.010
small thinkers are experts at doing? Here's
what it is. They are experts EXPERTS, they're

00:00:53.010 --> 00:01:00.670
incredibly, incredibly good at justifying
every single situation that happened to them.

00:01:00.670 --> 00:01:03.800
Now don't get me wrong. I know this from experience.
I'm not telling you this as if I'm talking

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:07.979
at you. I did this for the majority part of
my life. Hey, Pat, the reason you're not going

00:01:07.979 --> 00:01:12.200
to win is because you come from a divorced
family. You are this, you don't have a degree.

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:17.390
Constantly justifying. Well, because of this,
because of that. Not effective; it doesn't

00:01:17.390 --> 00:01:21.409
work, right? So, I sat there and I said, I
was mentoring a couple of entrepreneurs and

00:01:21.409 --> 00:01:23.930
I was sitting down with them. And I said,
"Can I ask you a question?" I was studying

00:01:23.930 --> 00:01:29.640
one of the guy's trends who's got the potential
to make multi-seven figures, but I noticed

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:34.760
a certain trend. And I pulled him aside and
I said, "Can I ask you a question?" He said

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:40.650
yes. I said, "Can I ask why you justify every
single setback? Why do you do that? Why do

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:44.770
you sell to everybody? Because you can do
that every single time. Why is it that you

00:01:44.770 --> 00:01:49.530
explain to everybody that what happened wasn't
your fault. And it's because of this and it's

00:01:49.530 --> 00:01:56.810
because of that. Why do you do that?" And
I had this conversation because 16 years ago,

00:01:56.810 --> 00:01:59.340
somebody had a very similar conversation like
this with me. And let me tell you that it

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:06.799
was the most annoying conversation I ever
had because we hate, listen, we hate to know

00:02:06.799 --> 00:02:11.039
that something that happened was our fault.
We hate it. When a relationship breakup happens,

00:02:11.039 --> 00:02:16.220
it's never our fault. It's always her fault.
When a relationship --it's never her fault;

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:21.720
it's his fault. When a business doesn't work,
it's the business' fault. It's the boss, it's

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:25.400
this, it's the team. It's always that. So
how do you find that if this is a trend that

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:30.250
you have or not. So let me give you three
different scenarios and I want you to answer

00:02:30.250 --> 00:02:32.340
to yourself on how you respond to this.

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:38.580
So #1, first question, let's study this. How
do you respond to setbacks? What is your immediate

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:43.610
reaction to setbacks? So think about the last
two or three setbacks. What did you do? Truly

00:02:43.610 --> 00:02:51.200
be honest with yourself. Did you blame anybody?
Did you blame the marketplace? Did you blame

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:57.420
a condition? Did you blame anything? Was there
anything? Or did you say, "Here's what happened.

00:02:57.420 --> 00:03:01.200
I don't control this. I take responsibility
on this. I can work on this. This is what

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:04.459
I'm going to improve." Is that what you did?
Is that the behavior?

00:03:04.459 --> 00:03:09.120
#2: Say you have a competitor that's doing
better than you. They're making more money

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:14.060
than you in a shorter period of time. Let's
just say this person is marketing better or

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:18.069
they're getting into new markets and you're
not. What is your reaction to that and how

00:03:18.069 --> 00:03:22.840
do you explain that to your peers? Is it,
"Man, they are doing this better than us?"

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:26.769
Or is it, "Oh man, they got lucky. That guy
comes from family. Oh, they're probably doing

00:03:26.769 --> 00:03:31.560
something shady. Oh, they're probably doing
something this. Oh they're. . ." You know,

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:37.659
we, because of. . . What do you do? How do
you react to it? Is the conversation, "Man,

00:03:37.659 --> 00:03:40.830
we need to improve in these three areas. We
really need to. Guys, what do you think we

00:03:40.830 --> 00:03:44.599
need to do to improve these two areas? These
guys are doing it this way. Man, we need to

00:03:44.599 --> 00:03:48.450
be seeing what they're doing and we're not
doing. Are they working harder." I remember

00:03:48.450 --> 00:03:56.940
one time, one time, I'll tell you this story.
I was going against this other agent.

00:03:56.940 --> 00:04:02.400
And he had been there a shorter period of
time than I had. And he grew ten times faster

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:06.659
than me in a two year period. And he just
came and surpassed me during that period.

00:04:06.659 --> 00:04:16.280
I'll never forget this. We're at a convention
and the executives made me at this time give

00:04:16.280 --> 00:04:20.529
the trophy to the other guy. I'll never forget
this. I give the trophy to the other guy because

00:04:20.529 --> 00:04:26.719
he won MVP and I didn't. And they wanted for
whatever reason, maybe I was going to be humiliated

00:04:26.719 --> 00:04:33.509
or something like that, and I will remember
that 35-second scene in my life forever. Because

00:04:33.509 --> 00:04:36.770
let me tell you what was crazy and what I
told myself when I got off the stage. Here's

00:04:36.770 --> 00:04:41.330
what I told myself. The strangest thing. I
got off the stage and I say, "Wow, Pat, I'm

00:04:41.330 --> 00:04:47.069
proud of you. You grew. You grew." Because
this was my response. I gave him the plaque

00:04:47.069 --> 00:04:52.349
and I said, "I just want you to know this.
You have absolutely outworked me. But this

00:04:52.349 --> 00:04:56.069
will be the last year. You won't even come
close. Because this will be the last time

00:04:56.069 --> 00:04:59.999
you will ever outwork me. Because I know I
think big and I know I'm clear about what

00:04:59.999 --> 00:05:04.869
I want to do in my life, but this is what
happened here's to you. Enjoy this celebration

00:05:04.869 --> 00:05:11.189
tonight. It's absolutely your show." No animosity.
No issues. No nothing. Came back that night,

00:05:11.189 --> 00:05:18.909
pages and pages of notes that night. Okay?
On what I did wrong. On what was my mistake.

00:05:18.909 --> 00:05:24.389
On what I stopped doing. I was not reading.
I was not working. I had let my body go. I

00:05:24.389 --> 00:05:29.689
was not exercising. I was not edgy. I was
not staying on point with certain areas to

00:05:29.689 --> 00:05:34.129
improve. I was not marketing as good. There
were certain things I was not happy about.

00:05:34.129 --> 00:05:39.740
I made a list of it. Boom. The rest is history.
You can't even compare the numbers now, but

00:05:39.740 --> 00:05:43.349
the rest is history. Because we had to improve.
Now by the way, there's a lot of other people

00:05:43.349 --> 00:05:45.869
that are going to do it better than you, faster
than you, all that other. This doesn't mean

00:05:45.869 --> 00:05:51.259
you're going to beat everybody. You can do
this assessment and your name is Clyde Drexler

00:05:51.259 --> 00:05:54.879
and you're probably never going to be better
than Michael Jordan. You can do this assessment

00:05:54.879 --> 00:05:58.589
and you may be Lamar Odom and you're never
going to be better than Kobe Bryant. But hey,

00:05:58.589 --> 00:06:03.499
why not reach your capacity? Why not get better
than what you are today? Why do you keep justifying?

00:06:03.499 --> 00:06:07.919
Why do you keep saying all these sorts of
things? Until a small thinker doesn't identify

00:06:07.919 --> 00:06:13.569
this and address it, I hate to say it to you,
not a lot's going to change. But it all starts

00:06:13.569 --> 00:06:18.659
with you realizing what I said at the beginning
of the video: nobody is a bigger enemy to

00:06:18.659 --> 00:06:24.199
you, not the enemy, not the competition, not
the client, not the industry, nobody is a

00:06:24.199 --> 00:06:29.360
bigger enemy to you than the way you think.If
you can fix the thinking here, the world is

00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:35.050
yours. That's the biggest challenge for small
thinking entrepreneurs. So, that's my message

00:06:35.050 --> 00:06:39.020
to you. If you have any questions or thoughts
about it, comment on the bottom, and if you

